Object.assign((window.PYQ_EXPLAIN = window.PYQ_EXPLAIN || {}), {"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-1":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Tautology = har truth assignment pe True. Jis formula ko False karne wala ek bhi row mil jaaye, wo tautology nahi.","concept":"### Tautology check karne ke 2 tareeke\n1. **Truth table** — sab 2^n rows.\n2. **Falsify karne ki koshish** — implication X → Y sirf tab False jab X = T aur Y = F. Agar aisa assignment possible nahi → tautology.\n### Yaad rakhne wale tautologies\n- Simplification: (p ∧ q) → p\n- Addition: p → (p ∨ q)\n- Hypothetical syllogism: ((p→q) ∧ (q→r)) → (p→r)\n- Modus ponens: (p ∧ (p → q)) → q\n- (p → q) ∨ (q → p) hamesha True (dono ek saath False nahi ho sakte).","tips":"- Implication ko False banane ke liye \"LHS = T, RHS = F\" try karo — 10 second me jawab.","sol":"- (A) Dono implications ek saath False ke liye p = T, q = F (pehla) aur q = T, p = F (doosra) chahiye — contradiction → **tautology**.\n- (B) LHS T ⇒ p = T ⇒ RHS T → **tautology**.\n- (C) p = T, q = F: LHS T, RHS F → **not** tautology.\n- (D) Hypothetical syllogism → **tautology**.\n- (E) p = F, q = T: LHS T, RHS F → **not** tautology.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 1.3 (Propositional equivalences), Sec 1.6 (Rules of inference)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-2":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Selective Repeat: window ≤ 2^(m−1). Go-Back-N: window ≤ 2^m − 1.","concept":"### Sliding window protocols\n- **Stop-and-Wait**: window 1 (sequence numbers 0, 1).\n- **Go-Back-N**: sender window ≤ 2^m − 1, receiver window = 1; error par us frame se aage sab dobara.\n- **Selective Repeat**: sender window = receiver window ≤ 2^(m−1); sirf kharab frame dobara.\n- Window zyada badi ho to purane aur naye frames ke sequence number confuse hote (duplicate accept).\n### Efficiency\n- η = W / (1 + 2a) agar W < 1 + 2a, warna 1; a = Tp / Tt.","tips":"- 4 bits → 16 numbers: GBN 15, SR 8.","sol":"m = 4 → Selective Repeat window ≤ 2^(4−1) = **8**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 8**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 11.5 (Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat ARQ)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-3":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"A kisi FD ke right side me nahi → har key me A. A ke saath B, C, D, E me se ek-ek jod ke closure dekho.","concept":"### Candidate key nikaalna\n1. Jo attribute kisi RHS me nahi → har key me hoga.\n2. Unka closure nikaalo; poora R na mile to baaki attributes ek-ek jod ke try karo.\n3. Minimal hona chahiye (koi proper subset superkey na ho).\n### Closure (X⁺) algorithm\nX⁺ = X; jab tak badle: har FD Y → Z me agar Y ⊆ X⁺ to Z jodo.","tips":"- Cycle dhoondho: AB → C → D → B. Is cycle ke B, C, D me se koi bhi A ke saath key bana sakta hai.","sol":"A⁺ = {A} → kaafi nahi.\n- AB: AB→C → C→D → D→E → ABCDE ✓ key.\n- AC: C→D → D→B, D→E → ABCDE ✓ key.\n- AD: D→B, D→E → AB→C → ABCDE ✓ key.\n- AE: E se kuch nahi milta → nahi.\n- BD (option D) me A nahi hai → key ho hi nahi sakta.\nKeys = AB, AC, AD.\n\n**Answer: (C) AB, AC and AD**","refs":"- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 14.2–14.3 (Closure, keys).\n- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 7.4.1."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-4":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pages = 2^32 / 2^12 = 2^20. Page table size = entries × entry size.","concept":"### Paging formulas\n- Offset bits = log₂(page size).\n- Page number bits = logical address bits − offset bits → entries = 2^(page bits).\n- Page table size = entries × PTE size.\n- Frames = physical memory / page size; PTE me frame number + control bits (valid, dirty, protection).\n- **Multilevel paging**: page table bada ho to usko bhi pages me todo.\n- **EAT with TLB** = h(t + m) + (1 − h)(t + 2m).","tips":"- 2^20 × 2^2 = 2^22 bytes = 4 MB.","sol":"Offset = 12 bits (4 KB). Page number bits = 32 − 12 = 20 → 2^20 entries.\nSize = 2^20 × 4 B = 2^22 B = **4 MB**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 4 MB**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 9.3 (Paging), Sec 9.4 (Structure of the page table)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-5":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"A: n^(log₂3) ≈ n^1.585 > n → case 1. D: n = 2^m rakho → S(m) = S(m/2) + 1.","concept":"### Master theorem: T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n)\n1. f(n) polynomially chhota → Θ(n^(log_b a))\n2. f(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a)) → Θ(n^(log_b a) log n)\n3. f(n) polynomially bada (+ regularity) → Θ(f(n))\n### Change of variable\nT(n) = T(√n) + 1: n = 2^m → S(m) = S(m/2) + 1 = Θ(log m) = Θ(log log n).\nT(n) = 2T(√n) + log n → Θ(log n · log log n).\n### Subtract-type\nT(n) = T(n−1) + c → Θ(n); T(n−1) + n → Θ(n²); 2T(n−1) + 1 → Θ(2^n).","tips":"- 3T(n/2) + n = Karatsuba multiplication ka recurrence.","sol":"- (A) n^(log₂3) vs n → case 1 → Θ(n^(log₂3)) → (III)\n- (B) Θ(n) → (IV)\n- (C) n³ vs n² → case 1 → Θ(n³) → (I)\n- (D) Θ(log log n) → (II)\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 4.5 (Master method), Sec 4.3 (changing variables)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-6":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"`static` local variable sirf ek baar initialize hota aur calls ke beech value yaad rakhta: f() → 2, 4, 6.","concept":"### Storage classes in C\n- **auto**: default local; har call pe naya; garbage initial value.\n- **static (local)**: ek baar initialize (0 default), lifetime poora program, scope function tak.\n- **static (global)**: file scope tak limited (internal linkage).\n- **extern**: doosri file me defined variable ka declaration.\n- **register**: CPU register ka request; address (&) nahi le sakte.","tips":"- Agar `static` hata do to har call 2 deti → s = 6 (option A trap).","sol":"Calls: c = 2, 4, 6 → s = 2 + 4 + 6 = **12**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 4.6 (Static variables), Sec 4.9 (Initialization)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-7":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"FP = UFP × (0.65 + 0.01 × ΣFi).","concept":"### Function Point Analysis (Albrecht)\n1. 5 components gino: EI (external inputs), EO (outputs), EQ (inquiries), ILF (internal logical files), EIF (external interface files).\n2. Simple/average/complex weights se **UFP**.\n3. 14 GSCs, har ek 0–5 → ΣFi (0 se 70).\n4. **VAF = 0.65 + 0.01 ΣFi** (range 0.65 – 1.35).\n5. **FP = UFP × VAF**.\n- Average weights: EI 4, EO 5, EQ 4, ILF 10, EIF 7.","tips":"- ΣFi = 35 → VAF = 1 (neutral). 42 > 35 → FP thoda bada.","sol":"VAF = 0.65 + 0.42 = 1.07. FP = 300 × 1.07 = **321**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 321**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 30.2.1 (Function-based metrics)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-8":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"5-bit range: −16 se +15. Decimal me badal ke jodo; result range se bahar = overflow. Opposite sign numbers jodne pe overflow kabhi nahi.","concept":"### Overflow in two's complement\n- n bits range: −2^(n−1) … 2^(n−1) − 1.\n- Overflow tabhi jab **dono operands same sign** ke hon aur result ka sign alag aaye.\n- Hardware check: V = C_in(MSB) ⊕ C_out(MSB).\n- Carry out (unsigned overflow) ≠ signed overflow.\n- (Ye GATE 2022 me 4-bit registers ke saath puchha gaya tha aur UGC NET Dec 2022 me repeat hua — isliye ye concept high-probability hai.)","tips":"- Pehle sign dekho: opposite signs (D) → seedha \"no overflow\".","sol":"- (A) 11 + 6 = 17 > 15 → **overflow** ✓\n- (B) −8 + (−4) = −12 → ok ✗\n- (C) −11 + (−6) = −17 < −16 → **overflow** ✓\n- (D) 15 + (−15) = 0 → ok ✗\n- (E) 7 + 8 = 15 → ok ✗\n\n**Answer: (A) (A) and (C) Only**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 3.3 (Arithmetic addition and overflow).\n- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 10.3 (Integer arithmetic, overflow rule)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-9":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Remainder-DFA me 6 states (0–5) bante, par minimization ke baad kuch merge ho jaate: 6 = 2 × 3 → (states for 3) + (1 for the factor 2).","concept":"### Divisibility DFA\n- State r = ab tak padhe number ka remainder mod k; bit b padhne par r' = (2r + b) mod k.\n- Start = final = state 0.\n- Minimum states (binary): k odd → k; k = 2^m → m + 1; k = 2^m·j (j odd) → j + m.\n- Isliye: div by 5 → 5, by 4 → 3, by 6 → 4, by 8 → 4, by 12 → 5.","tips":"- Remainders 1 aur 4 (mod 6) aur 2 aur 5 ek jaise behave karte → merge.","sol":"6 = 2¹ × 3 → minimum states = 3 + 1 = **4**.\n(Check: remainder states {1, 4} aur {2, 5} equivalent nikalte hain → 6 − 2 = 4.)\n\n**Answer: (C) 4**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 2.2 (DFA), Sec 4.4 (Minimization)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-10":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"A*: f(n) = g(n) + h(n). Greedy sirf h(n) dekhta → optimal guarantee nahi.","concept":"### Informed search\n- **Greedy best-first**: f = h; fast par complete/optimal nahi (tree search).\n- **A***: f = g + h. Admissible h (h ≤ h*) → tree search optimal. Consistent h (h(n) ≤ c(n,n') + h(n')) → graph search optimal.\n- h = 0 → f = g → **uniform cost search** (Dijkstra jaisa).\n- Dominance: h₂ ≥ h₁ (dono admissible) → h₂ kam nodes expand karta.\n- 8-puzzle heuristics: misplaced tiles (h₁), Manhattan distance (h₂, better).","tips":"- \"Always optimal\" + greedy = galat.","sol":"(A), (B), (C) sahi. (D) galat — greedy best-first optimal nahi.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 3.5 (Informed search: greedy, A*), Sec 3.6 (Heuristic functions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-11":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Handshaking se edges, phir Euler formula v − e + r = 2.","concept":"### Planar graph formulas\n- **Euler**: v − e + r = 2 (connected planar). k components → v − e + r = k + 1.\n- Simple planar (v ≥ 3): e ≤ 3v − 6. Triangle-free: e ≤ 2v − 4.\n- K₅ aur K₃,₃ non-planar (Kuratowski).\n- Har planar graph 4-colourable.","tips":"- Σdeg = 2e → 30 = 2e → e = 15.","sol":"e = (10 × 3)/2 = 15. r = e − v + 2 = 15 − 10 + 2 = **7**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 7**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 10.7 (Planar graphs, Euler's formula)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-12":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"σ = rows (horizontal), π = columns (vertical), ÷ = \"all\" queries.","concept":"### Relational algebra\n- **Basic**: σ, π, ∪, −, ×, ρ (rename). Baaki (∩, ⋈, ÷) inse derive hote.\n- σ_c(R) → |result| ≤ |R|. π duplicates hata deta (set semantics).\n- R ⋈ S = π(σ(R × S)) — common attributes pe equality.\n- R(A, B) ÷ S(B) = jo A values S ke **sab** B values ke saath aati hain. Example: \"un students ke naam jo **sab** courses lete hain\".\n- R ∩ S = R − (R − S).","tips":"- \"For all / every\" query dikhe → division.","sol":"σ → (III), π → (I), ⋈ → (IV), ÷ → (II).\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 6.1 (Relational algebra).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Ch 8."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-13":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"6 subnets → 3 subnet bits (2³ = 8 ≥ 6). Prefix /24 + 3 = /27.","concept":"### Subnetting steps\n1. Subnets s chahiye → subnet bits k = ⌈log₂ s⌉.\n2. Naya prefix = old + k.\n3. Hosts per subnet = 2^(32 − prefix) − 2.\n4. Mask last octet: /25 = 128, /26 = 192, /27 = 224, /28 = 240, /29 = 248, /30 = 252.\n- Max hosts ke liye subnet bits **kam se kam** rakho.","tips":"- /27 → block size 32 → 30 usable hosts per subnet.","sol":"k = 3 → /27 → mask 255.255.255.224, 8 subnets, 30 hosts each.\n\n**Answer: (C) 255.255.255.224**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 18.4 (Subnetting)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-14":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"#include / #define pehle (preprocessor) → compiler → assembler → linker → loader memory me daalta → CPU chalata.","concept":"### Language processing system\n- **Preprocessor**: macros expand, header files include, conditional compilation.\n- **Compiler**: source → assembly / object.\n- **Assembler**: assembly → relocatable object code (two-pass: pass 1 symbol table, pass 2 code).\n- **Linker**: object files + libraries → executable; external references resolve.\n- **Loader**: executable ko memory me laana, relocation, execution start.\n- **Dynamic linking**: library run time pe link (DLL / .so).","tips":"- Loader hamesha execution se theek pehle; linker loader se pehle.","sol":"E (preprocessing) → A (compilation) → B (linking) → C (loading) → D (execution).\n\n**Answer: (A) (E), (A), (B), (C), (D)**","refs":"- Leland L. Beck — System Software: An Introduction to Systems Programming, Ch 2 (Assemblers), Ch 3 (Loaders and linkers).\n- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers, Sec 1.1 (Language processors)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-15":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"V(G) = E − N + 2P.","concept":"### McCabe's cyclomatic complexity\n- **V(G) = E − N + 2P** (P = connected components).\n- **V(G) = number of predicate (decision) nodes + 1**.\n- **V(G) = number of regions** (planar CFG, outer region included).\n- Meaning: independent paths ki sankhya → basis path testing me minimum test cases.\n- V(G) > 10 → module complex, refactor socho.","tips":"- Teeno formula ek doosre ko cross-check karte hain.","sol":"V(G) = 10 − 8 + 2(1) = **4**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 4**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 23.4 (Basis path testing, cyclomatic complexity)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-16":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Size = pixels × bits per pixel / 8 bytes.","concept":"### Frame buffer\n- Size (bits) = resolution × bit depth. Bytes = /8.\n- Bit depth n → 2^n colours (24-bit = true colour, 16.7 million).\n- **Refresh**: raster scan me har frame poora redraw; access time per pixel = 1 / (pixels × refresh rate).\n- **Aspect ratio** = width : height.\n- Colour lookup table: frame buffer me index, LUT me actual colour.","tips":"- 1024 × 768 = 786432 pixels; × 3 bytes = 2359296 bytes = 2.25 × 2^20.","sol":"Bytes = 1024 × 768 × 3 = 2,359,296 B = 2.25 MB (1 MB = 2^20 B).\n\n**Answer: (C) 2.25 MB**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 2.1 (Raster-scan systems, frame buffer)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-17":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Offset = log₂(64) = 6, index = log₂(32K/64 = 512) = 9, tag = 32 − 6 − 9.","concept":"### Cache address split\n- **Direct mapped**: Tag | Index (line) | Offset.\n- **k-way set associative**: Tag | Set | Offset; sets = lines / k.\n- **Fully associative**: Tag | Offset.\n- Tag bits = address bits − log₂(cache size ÷ associativity) = address bits − set bits − offset bits.\n- Tag memory = lines × (tag + valid + dirty bits).\n- Associativity double karo → tag bits +1.","tips":"- Direct-mapped me tag = address bits − log₂(cache size): 32 − 15 = 17.","sol":"Offset = 6, lines = 2^15 / 2^6 = 2^9 → index = 9. Tag = 32 − 9 − 6 = **17**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 17**","refs":"- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 4.3 (Cache mapping functions).\n- Hamacher — Computer Organization, Sec 5.5."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-18":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Preorder ka pehla = root (A). Inorder me A ke left = left subtree, right = right subtree. Recursively.","concept":"### Tree reconstruction\n- Preorder + inorder → unique binary tree. Postorder + inorder → unique.\n- Preorder + postorder → unique sirf **full** binary tree ke liye.\n- Traversals: Pre = Root L R, In = L Root R, Post = L R Root.\n- Level order = BFS (queue).\n- BST ka inorder = sorted order.","tips":"- Postorder ka last element hamesha root — option ka last letter A hona chahiye.","sol":"Root A; left {D, B, E}, right {F, C, G}.\nLeft: preorder B D E → root B, left D, right E. Right: preorder C F G → root C, left F, right G.\nPostorder: D E B, F G C, A → **D E B F G C A**.\n\n**Answer: (A) D E B F G C A**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 12.1 (tree walks).\n- Seymour Lipschutz — Data Structures (Schaum's), Ch 7 (Trees)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-19":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Power: LL(1) ⊂ LR(1); SLR ⊂ LALR ⊂ CLR. Ambiguous grammar kisi bhi LL/LR class me nahi.","concept":"### Parser hierarchy\n- Bottom-up power: LR(0) < SLR(1) < LALR(1) < CLR(1).\n- **States**: LR(0) = SLR = LALR (same core); CLR zyada (lookaheads alag).\n- LALR merge karne se **reduce-reduce** conflict aa sakta, shift-reduce nahi (jo CLR me na ho).\n- LL(1) ⊂ LR(1); har LL(1) grammar LALR(1) bhi hona zaroori nahi.\n- Ambiguous grammar → kisi deterministic parser ki table me conflict.\n- Operator precedence grammar: koi ε-production nahi, do adjacent non-terminals nahi.","tips":"- \"Ambiguous\" + \"LL(1)/LR(k)\" → hamesha False.","sol":"- (A) Sahi.\n- (B) Sahi — LALR aur SLR dono LR(0) item-set cores pe.\n- (C) Sahi.\n- (D) Galat.\n- (E) Galat.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 4.6–4.7 (SLR, canonical LR, LALR), Sec 4.4.3 (LL(1))."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-20":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"μ_B'(x2) = 1 − 0.4 = 0.6. Intersection = min(0.7, 0.6).","concept":"### Standard fuzzy operations\n- Union: μ_{A∪B} = max(μA, μB)\n- Intersection: μ_{A∩B} = min(μA, μB)\n- Complement: μ_{A'} = 1 − μA\n- Fuzzy sets me **law of excluded middle fail**: A ∪ A' ≠ X (μ = 0.5 → max = 0.5).\n- **α-cut**: {x | μ(x) ≥ α}. **Support**: μ > 0. **Core**: μ = 1. **Height**: max μ.\n- Algebraic product: μA·μB; algebraic sum: μA + μB − μAμB.","tips":"- Pehle complement, phir min — order mat ulto.","sol":"μ_B'(x2) = 0.6; μ_{A∩B'}(x2) = min(0.7, 0.6) = **0.6**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 0.6**","refs":"- Timothy J. Ross — Fuzzy Logic with Engineering Applications, Ch 2 (Classical sets and fuzzy sets).\n- S. N. Sivanandam — Principles of Soft Computing, Ch 7."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-21":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Graphical method: optimum hamesha feasible region ke corner (vertex) pe. Corners nikaalo aur Z evaluate karo.","concept":"### LP graphical method\n1. Har constraint ko equality maan ke line khencho.\n2. Feasible region (sab constraints ka common area).\n3. Corner points (intersections) nikaalo.\n4. Z har corner pe → max/min.\n- Do corners pe same optimum → **multiple (infinite) optimal solutions** (objective line ek edge ke parallel).\n- Region unbounded aur Z badhta jaaye → unbounded solution.\n- Simplex: slack variables (≤), surplus + artificial (≥); Big-M / two-phase.","tips":"- Intersection: (x + y = 4) − (x + 3y = 6) → y = 1, x = 3.","sol":"Corners: (0,0), (4,0), (3,1), (0,2).\nZ: 0, **12**, 11, 4. Max = 12 at (4, 0).\n\n**Answer: (C) 12**","refs":"- Hamdy A. Taha — Operations Research: An Introduction, Sec 2.2 (Graphical LP solution)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-22":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"2PL = growing phase + shrinking phase → serializable, par deadlock ho sakta. Conservative 2PL sab locks pehle hi le leta → deadlock nahi.","concept":"### 2PL variants\n- **Basic**: lock point ke baad koi naya lock nahi. Conflict serializable; deadlock + cascading rollback possible.\n- **Strict 2PL**: X-locks commit/abort tak → cascadeless (strict schedules). Deadlock possible.\n- **Rigorous 2PL**: sab locks (S + X) commit tak → serial order = commit order.\n- **Conservative (static) 2PL**: start se pehle sab locks → **deadlock-free**, par practical nahi.\n### Other protocols\n- Timestamp ordering: deadlock-free, starvation possible. Thomas write rule → view serializable.","tips":"- \"Deadlock-free\" sirf conservative 2PL ke saath sahi.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗, (C) ✓, (D) ✓, (E) ✗ (conservative 2PL deadlock-free).\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 18.1 (Lock-based protocols, 2PL variants).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 21.1."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-23":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"n = 77, φ(n) = 6 × 10 = 60. d = e⁻¹ mod 60 → 13d ≡ 1 (mod 60).","concept":"### RSA steps\n1. Primes p, q; n = pq; φ(n) = (p − 1)(q − 1).\n2. e chuno: 1 < e < φ, gcd(e, φ) = 1.\n3. d = e⁻¹ mod φ (extended Euclid).\n4. Encrypt C = M^e mod n; decrypt M = C^d mod n.\n- Public key (e, n), private key (d, n).\n- Security: n ko factor karna kathin.\n- Digital signature: S = M^d mod n, verify M = S^e mod n.","tips":"- Options se check: 13 × 37 = 481 = 8 × 60 + 1 ✓.","sol":"φ = 60. 13 × 37 = 481 ≡ 1 (mod 60) → **d = 37**.\n(Check others: 13 × 23 = 299 ≡ 59, 13 × 47 = 611 ≡ 11, 13 × 53 = 689 ≡ 29.)\n\n**Answer: (C) 37**","refs":"- William Stallings — Cryptography and Network Security, Sec 9.2 (The RSA algorithm).\n- Forouzan — Cryptography and Network Security, Ch 10."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-24":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Address → tag compare → miss → main memory se block → cache me rakho → word CPU ko.","concept":"### Cache operation\n- **Hit**: tag match + valid bit → word seedha cache se.\n- **Miss**: block (cache line) main memory se laao; set full ho to **replacement** (LRU, FIFO, random).\n- Write policies: **write-through** (cache + memory dono; write buffer) vs **write-back** (sirf cache; dirty bit; replace pe memory me).\n- Write miss: write-allocate vs no-write-allocate.\n- Miss types (3 C's): compulsory, capacity, conflict.\n- Early restart / critical word first: word aate hi CPU ko.","tips":"- Tag compare ke bina miss pata hi nahi chalta → B, C se pehle.","sol":"A (address) → B (tag compare) → C (miss, fetch block) → D (place block) → E (deliver word).\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B), (C), (D), (E)**","refs":"- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 4.2–4.3 (Cache read operation, Fig 4.5).\n- Hamacher — Computer Organization, Sec 5.5."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-25":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Overload nahi hote: `::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:`, `sizeof`. Sirf member se: `=`, `()`, `[]`, `->`. Naya operator nahi bana sakte.","concept":"### Operator overloading rules\n- Syntax: `T operator+(const T&)`.\n- Member: left operand object; friend: dono operands parameters (jaise `ostream& operator<<(ostream&, const T&)`).\n- Arity, precedence, associativity same rehti hain.\n- Not overloadable: `::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:`, `sizeof`, `typeid`.\n- Only-member: `=`, `()`, `[]`, `->`.\n- Kam se kam ek operand user-defined type ka hona chahiye.","tips":"- \"Naya operator\" ya \"precedence badalna\" → hamesha galat.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✓, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Bjarne Stroustrup — The C++ Programming Language, Ch 18 (Operator overloading).\n- E. Balagurusamy — Object Oriented Programming with C++, Ch 7."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-26":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Spiral = risk; Prototype = unclear requirements; RAD = 60–90 din, components; Waterfall = linear.","concept":"### Process models — ek line me\n- **Waterfall** (classic life cycle): linear; har phase ka output agle ka input; late feedback.\n- **V-model**: har dev phase ke saamne test phase.\n- **Incremental**: core product pehle, baaki increments.\n- **Prototyping**: quick design → customer feedback → refine.\n- **Spiral** (Boehm): iterations + har loop me **risk analysis**; bade/risky projects.\n- **RAD**: very short cycle (60–90 days), reusable components, multiple teams.\n- **Agile**: XP, Scrum.","tips":"- Keyword mapping hi poora sawaal hai — \"risk\" → spiral, \"fuzzy\" → prototype.","sol":"A → II, B → I, C → IV, D → III.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 4.1 (Prescriptive process models)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-27":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Do sabse chhote frequencies ko baar-baar merge karo. Total bits = sab merged (internal) nodes ke weights ka sum.","concept":"### Huffman coding (greedy)\n1. Har character ek node (frequency = weight), min-priority queue.\n2. Do minimum nikaalo, merge (sum), wapas daalo. n − 1 merges.\n3. Left = 0, right = 1 → prefix-free codes.\n- Complexity O(n log n).\n- Shortcut: **total cost = sab merged (internal) nodes ke weights ka sum**.\n- Fixed-length code yahan 3 bits × 100 = 300.\n- Average code length = total bits / total frequency.","tips":"- Merges: 11, 21, 35, 56, 100 → sum 223.","sol":"Merges: 4 + 7 = 11; 10 + 11 = 21; 15 + 20 = 35; 21 + 35 = 56; 44 + 56 = 100.\nTotal = 11 + 21 + 35 + 56 + 100 = **223**.\n(Code lengths: f 1; c, d, e 3; a, b 4 → 44 + 3(10 + 15 + 20) + 4(4 + 7) = 44 + 135 + 44 = 223. Average = 2.23 bits/char.)\n\n**Answer: (A) 223**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 16.3 (Huffman codes)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-28":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har step pe current head se sabse nazdeek request: 50 → 43 → 24 → 16 → 82 → 140 → 170 → 190.","concept":"### Disk scheduling\n- **FCFS**: queue order; fair, zyada movement.\n- **SSTF**: nearest first; starvation possible.\n- **SCAN (elevator)**: ek direction me end tak, phir ulta.\n- **C-SCAN**: ek direction me service, end se seedha start (return pe service nahi).\n- **LOOK / C-LOOK**: end tak nahi, last request tak.\n- Seek time dominant cost; total movement = Σ |differences|.","tips":"- Isi queue pe FCFS = 642 — option trap.","sol":"50→43 (7), →24 (19), →16 (8), →82 (66), →140 (58), →170 (30), →190 (20).\nTotal = 7 + 19 + 8 + 66 + 58 + 30 + 20 = **208**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 208**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 11.2 (HDD scheduling: FCFS, SSTF, SCAN, C-SCAN)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-29":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Ek stack ek hi comparison kar sakta. aⁿbⁿcⁿ me teen counts barabar chahiye.","concept":"### CFL ya non-CFL\n- CFL: aⁿbⁿ, wwᴿ (palindromes), aⁱbʲ (i ≠ j), aⁿbᵐcᵐdⁿ, balanced parentheses.\n- Non-CFL: aⁿbⁿcⁿ, ww, aⁿbᵐcⁿdᵐ, a^(n²), a^p (p prime).\n- Proof: **pumping lemma for CFL**: z = uvwxy, |vwx| ≤ p, |vx| ≥ 1, uvⁱwxⁱy ∈ L.\n- aⁿbⁿcⁿ context-sensitive hai.","tips":"- \"Teen ya zyada blocks equal\" ya \"copy (ww)\" → non-CFL.","sol":"aⁿbⁿcⁿ ke liye do independent comparisons chahiye → **not CFL** (pumping lemma). Baaki teeno PDA se accept.\n\n**Answer: (D) { aⁿbⁿcⁿ | n ≥ 0 }**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 7.2 (Pumping lemma for CFLs)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-30":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Order = smallest k with 3^k ≡ 1 (mod 7). Powers: 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, 1.","concept":"### Group theory basics\n- Group: closure, associativity, identity, inverse. Abelian = commutative bhi.\n- **Order of element** a: smallest k > 0 with a^k = e.\n- **Lagrange**: subgroup ka order group ke order ko divide karta → element ka order bhi.\n- **Cyclic group**: ek generator se poora group. Zₙ* cyclic for n = 1, 2, 4, p^k, 2p^k.\n- Cyclic group of order n me generators = φ(n).","tips":"- Order 6 = group order → 3 generator (primitive root mod 7).","sol":"3¹ = 3, 3² = 9 ≡ 2, 3³ = 6, 3⁴ = 18 ≡ 4, 3⁵ = 12 ≡ 5, 3⁶ = 15 ≡ 1. Order = **6**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 6**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 4.4 (Primitive roots).\n- I. N. Herstein — Topics in Algebra, Ch 2 (Groups, Lagrange's theorem)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-31":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Hub sirf signal (bits) repeat karta; switch MAC address dekhta; router IP address dekhta; gateway protocol conversion (sab layers).","concept":"### Connecting devices\n- **Repeater / Hub** (L1): signal regenerate; ek collision domain, ek broadcast domain.\n- **Bridge / Switch** (L2): MAC table, frames filter; har port alag collision domain; ek broadcast domain.\n- **Router** (L3): IP routing; har interface alag broadcast domain.\n- **Layer-3 switch**: switching + routing.\n- **Gateway** (L1–L7): alag protocols wale networks jodta (protocol converter).\n### Collision vs broadcast domains\n24-port switch → 24 collision domains, 1 broadcast domain (VLAN na ho to).","tips":"- \"Domains\" wale numericals bhi isi se aate hain.","sol":"Hub → Physical (III), Switch → Data link (IV), Router → Network (I), Gateway → all layers (II).\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch 17 (Connecting devices)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-32":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"NoSQL ke 4 types: key-value (Redis), document (MongoDB), column-family (Cassandra, HBase), graph (Neo4j).","concept":"### NoSQL\n- **Key-value**: Redis, DynamoDB — schema-less, simple get/put, joins nahi.\n- **Document**: MongoDB, CouchDB — JSON/BSON documents.\n- **Column-family (wide-column)**: Cassandra, HBase, Bigtable.\n- **Graph**: Neo4j — nodes + edges (social networks).\n### CAP theorem (Brewer)\nNetwork partition ke time Consistency ya Availability me se ek chhodna padta → CP (HBase, MongoDB default) ya AP (Cassandra, DynamoDB).\n### ACID vs BASE\nRDBMS → ACID (strong consistency); NoSQL → BASE (eventual consistency, high availability).","tips":"- Cassandra = column-family, Neo4j = graph — ye pair yaad rakho.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗ (Cassandra wide-column), (D) ✓, (E) ✗ (schema-less, joins nahi).\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems (7th ed.), Ch 24 (NoSQL databases and big data storage systems)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-33":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Static member function ke paas `this` pointer nahi → sirf static members. Destructor ek hi (overload nahi).","concept":"### C++ facts jo baar-baar puchhe jaate\n- **friend**: class ke private/protected access; member nahi hota; friendship inherit/transitive nahi.\n- **static member function**: `this` nahi; sirf static data/functions; `Class::f()` se call.\n- **Destructor**: ek per class, no args, no return, virtual ho sakta.\n- **Abstract class**: pure virtual function; object nahi banta, par constructor ho sakta (derived ke construction me chalta).\n- **Virtual function** + base pointer/reference → late (dynamic) binding via vtable.\n- Function overloading / templates → compile-time polymorphism.","tips":"- \"this pointer nahi\" = static member function ka sabse common trap.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗, (C) ✗, (D) ✓, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (D) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Bjarne Stroustrup — The C++ Programming Language, Sec 16.2.12 (static members), Sec 19.4 (friends), Sec 20.4 (abstract classes)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-34":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Minterms dekho: 1, 3, 9, 11 me B = 0, D = 1; 4, 6, 12, 14 me B = 1, D = 0. To F = B'D + BD'.","concept":"### K-map tricks\n- 1, 3, 9, 11 (B = 0, D = 1) ek group = B'D. 4, 6, 12, 14 (B = 1, D = 0) = BD'.\n- **XOR**: x'y + xy' = x ⊕ y. **XNOR**: x'y' + xy = (x ⊕ y)'.\n- Group size 2^k; jo variable group me badle wo hata do.\n- 8 minterms in 4-var → result me 1 variable ya XOR/XNOR jaisa pattern.","tips":"- Function \"B aur D alag hon tab 1\" → XOR.","sol":"F = B'D + BD' = **B ⊕ D**.\n\n**Answer: (B) B ⊕ D**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 3.3 (Four-variable K-map), Sec 3.8 (Exclusive-OR functions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-35":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har fault pe us page ko nikaalo jiska last use sabse purana hai.","concept":"### Page replacement\n- **FIFO**: sabse purana loaded page bahar; **Belady's anomaly** (frames badhane par faults badh sakte).\n- **Optimal (OPT)**: jo sabse der baad use hoga; minimum faults (benchmark).\n- **LRU**: jiska last use sabse purana; stack algorithm → anomaly nahi.\n- LRU approximations: reference bit, second chance (clock), NRU.\n- Effective access time = (1 − p)·ma + p·page-fault service time.\n- Isi string pe: FIFO = 8, LRU = 7, OPT = 6.","tips":"- Table banao: frames + har page ka last-use time.","sol":"1 F [1]; 3 F [1,3]; 0 F [1,3,0]; 3 hit; 5 F (LRU = 1) [5,3,0]; 6 F (LRU = 0) [5,3,6]; 3 hit; 1 F (LRU = 5) [1,3,6]; 6 hit; 0 F (LRU = 3) [1,0,6].\nFaults = **7**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 7**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 10.4 (Page replacement: FIFO, optimal, LRU)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-36":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Plan karo → team ko overview do → har reviewer akela padhe → meeting me defects log → author rework → (follow-up).","concept":"### Fagan inspection (1976)\n1. **Planning**: material, team (moderator, author, reader, recorder), schedule.\n2. **Overview**: author product samjhata.\n3. **Preparation**: har inspector akela padh ke defects note.\n4. **Inspection meeting**: reader padhta, defects record (solve nahi karte, sirf log).\n5. **Rework**: author fix karta.\n6. **Follow-up**: moderator verify karta.\n### Formal Technical Review (Pressman)\n3–5 log, 2 ghante se kam meeting, product review (producer nahi); output: accept / reject / provisional accept.\n### Software quality\nSQA activities, McCall factors, ISO 9126 / 25010, CMM / CMMI levels, Six Sigma (DMAIC).","tips":"- Meeting se pehle preparation, meeting ke baad rework.","sol":"Planning (A) → Overview (B) → Preparation (C) → Inspection meeting (D) → Rework (E).\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B), (C), (D), (E)**","refs":"- Ian Sommerville — Software Engineering, Sec 24.3 (Reviews and inspections).\n- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Ch 20 (Review techniques)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-37":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"BFS sirf unweighted (ya equal-weight) graphs me shortest path deta.","concept":"### Shortest paths (GATE + UGC favourite)\n- **Dijkstra**: non-negative weights; binary heap O((V + E) log V); Fibonacci heap O(E + V log V); array O(V²).\n- **Bellman-Ford**: O(VE); negative edges OK; V-th pass me relax ho to negative cycle.\n- **Floyd-Warshall**: all pairs, O(V³), DP.\n- **BFS**: unweighted; O(V + E).\n- **DAG shortest path**: topological order + relax → O(V + E), negative edges bhi chalte.","tips":"- \"Any weighted graph\" + BFS → galat.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B), (C) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Ch 24–25."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-38":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Skolemization ∃ (existential) hataata hai. FOL entailment semi-decidable hai (decidable nahi).","concept":"### FOL inference\n- **Sound**: sirf sahi conclusions. **Complete**: har entailed sentence derive ho sake.\n- Resolution: sound + refutation-complete.\n- CNF conversion: → hatao, ¬ andar, standardize, **Skolemize (∃ → Skolem constant/function)**, ∀ drop, distribute.\n- FOL validity/entailment: **semi-decidable** (Church–Turing) — entailed hai to proof milega, nahi hai to kabhi ruk na bhi sake.\n- Propositional logic: decidable (truth table, NP-complete SAT).","tips":"- Skolem = ∃ ke liye; ∀ simply drop hota.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✓, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 9.1–9.2 (Propositional vs first-order inference, semi-decidability), Sec 9.5 (Resolution)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-39":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"6 letters: A × 3, N × 2, B × 1 → 6! / (3! 2!).","concept":"### Permutations with repetition\n- n objects, n₁, n₂, … identical groups → n! / (n₁! n₂! …).\n- **Combinations**: C(n, r) = n! / (r!(n − r)!).\n- Repetition allowed selection (stars and bars): C(n + r − 1, r).\n- Circular arrangements: (n − 1)!; necklace (flip same): (n − 1)!/2.\n- Derangements: D(n) = n! Σ (−1)^k / k!; D(4) = 9.","tips":"- \"A's together\" variant: AAA ek block → 4!/2! = 12.","sol":"6! / (3! × 2!) = 720 / 12 = **60**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 60**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 6.5 (Generalized permutations and combinations)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-40":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"HTTP 80, SMTP 25, DNS 53, FTP 21 (data 20).","concept":"### Port numbers (yaad rakho)\n- FTP 20 (data), 21 (control) — TCP\n- SSH 22, Telnet 23, SMTP 25 — TCP\n- DNS 53 — UDP (zone transfer TCP)\n- DHCP 67 (server) / 68 (client) — UDP\n- TFTP 69 — UDP; HTTP 80 — TCP; POP3 110; IMAP 143; SNMP 161/162 — UDP; HTTPS 443.\n- Well-known ports 0–1023; registered 1024–49151; dynamic 49152–65535.","tips":"- FTP **out-of-band** control (alag connection) — ye statement bhi aata hai.","sol":"HTTP → 80 (III), SMTP → 25 (IV), DNS → 53 (I), FTP control → 21 (II).\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch 24 (Transport layer, port numbers), Ch 26.\n- Kurose & Ross — Computer Networking, Ch 2."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-41":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Group-wise average: HR 55000, IT 51666.67, Sales 45000. HAVING groups ko filter karta.","concept":"### SQL query evaluation order\nFROM → WHERE (rows filter) → GROUP BY → HAVING (groups filter) → SELECT → DISTINCT → ORDER BY.\n- WHERE me aggregate (AVG, COUNT) nahi likh sakte; HAVING me likhte.\n- COUNT(*) NULLs bhi ginta; COUNT(col) NULL chhod deta. AVG/SUM NULL ignore.\n- SELECT me non-aggregated column GROUP BY me hona chahiye.","tips":"- IT avg = (60000 + 65000 + 30000)/3 = 51666.67 > 50000 — dhyan se divide karo.","sol":"HR: (40000 + 70000)/2 = 55000 ✓ count 2. IT: 155000/3 ≈ 51667 ✓ count 3. Sales: 45000 ✗.\nOutput: (HR, 2), (IT, 3).\n\n**Answer: (A) (HR, 2) and (IT, 3)**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 3.7 (Aggregate functions, GROUP BY, HAVING)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-42":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Interrupt instruction ke beech me nahi lagta — current instruction poora hota, phir state save, phir ISR, phir restore (RETI/IRET).","concept":"### Interrupt handling\n- **Vectored interrupt**: device ISR ka address (vector) deta. Non-vectored: fixed address.\n- Priority: daisy chaining (serial), parallel priority encoder.\n- **Maskable vs non-maskable** (NMI — power failure jaise).\n- Interrupt vs **trap** (software/exception) vs **polling**.\n- I/O modes: programmed I/O (busy wait) < interrupt-driven < DMA.","tips":"- Save state hamesha ISR se pehle, restore hamesha baad.","sol":"A (request) → B (current instruction complete) → C (save PC/PSW) → D (ISR) → E (restore + resume).\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B), (C), (D), (E)**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 11.5 (Priority interrupt), Sec 5.7 (Interrupt cycle).\n- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 3.2 (Interrupts)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-43":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Fixed-point scaling: x' = xf + sx(x − xf), y' = yf + sy(y − yf).","concept":"### 2-D transformations (homogeneous coordinates)\n- Translation: [1 0 tx; 0 1 ty; 0 0 1]\n- Scaling (origin): [sx 0 0; 0 sy 0; 0 0 1]\n- Rotation (origin, anticlockwise θ): [cosθ −sinθ 0; sinθ cosθ 0; 0 0 1]\n- **About a fixed/pivot point**: translate point to origin → transform → translate back: T(xf, yf)·S·T(−xf, −yf).\n- Reflection about x-axis: (x, −y); about y = x: (y, x).\n- Shear (x-direction): x' = x + shx·y.","tips":"- Origin ke about scale karte to (8, 3) aata — option A trap.","sol":"x' = 2 + 2(4 − 2) = 6; y' = 2 + 0.5(6 − 2) = 4 → **(6, 4)**.\n\n**Answer: (B) (6, 4)**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 5.1–5.3 (Basic and composite 2-D transformations)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-44":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har P −1, har V +1: 10 − 12 + 5.","concept":"### Semaphores\n- **wait(S)/P**: S−−; S < 0 → process block (|S| = waiting processes, blocking implementation).\n- **signal(S)/V**: S++; koi waiting ho to ek ko wake up.\n- **Binary semaphore** (0/1) ≈ mutex. **Counting** → n instances wala resource.\n- Classic problems: producer-consumer (mutex, empty, full), readers-writers, dining philosophers.\n- Deadlock example: P1: wait(S); wait(Q) | P2: wait(Q); wait(S).","tips":"- Final value order pe depend nahi karta (sirf net count).","sol":"S = 10 − 12 + 5 = **3**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 3**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 6.6 (Semaphores), Sec 7.1 (Classic problems)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-45":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Branch coverage ⇒ statement coverage (ulta nahi). Top-down me stubs, bottom-up me drivers.","concept":"### Testing techniques\n- **Black-box**: equivalence partitioning, boundary value analysis, cause-effect graph, decision table.\n- **White-box**: statement < branch (decision) < condition < path coverage (strength badhti); basis path (cyclomatic).\n- **Integration**: top-down (stubs), bottom-up (drivers), sandwich, big-bang.\n- **Alpha** (developer site, users ke saath) vs **Beta** (customer site, developer absent).\n- **Regression**: change ke baad purane tests dobara.\n- Verification (\"product sahi bana rahe?\") vs validation (\"sahi product bana rahe?\").","tips":"- Stub = called module ka dummy (upar se neeche jaate waqt); Driver = calling module ka dummy.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗ (ulta), (C) ✓, (D) ✓, (E) ✗ (drivers).\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Ch 22–23 (Testing strategies and techniques)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-46":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"π[i] = P[1..i] ka longest proper prefix jo suffix bhi ho. \"aabaa\" → \"aa\" (2); \"aabaaa\" → \"aa\" (2); \"aabaaab\" → \"aab\" (3).","concept":"### String matching algorithms\n- **Naive**: O((n − m + 1)m).\n- **Rabin-Karp**: rolling hash; average O(n + m), worst O(nm) (spurious hits).\n- **KMP**: prefix function O(m) + matching O(n) → **O(n + m)**; text pointer kabhi piche nahi jaata.\n- **Finite automaton matcher**: O(m|Σ|) preprocessing, O(n) matching.\n- **Boyer-Moore**: right-to-left, bad character + good suffix rules.","tips":"- 6th position pe \"aabaaa\": prefix \"aab\" ≠ suffix \"aaa\", isliye 3 nahi, 2.","sol":"a 0; aa 1; aab 0; aaba 1; aabaa 2; aabaaa 2; aabaaab 3.\nπ = **0, 1, 0, 1, 2, 2, 3**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 0, 1, 0, 1, 2, 2, 3**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 32.4 (Knuth-Morris-Pratt algorithm, prefix function)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-47":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Enum me bina value wala constant = pichhla + 1. BLUE = 6, BLACK = 3.","concept":"### Enumerations in C\n- Pehla constant default 0; har agla pichhle + 1.\n- Explicit value di to wahan se counting reset.\n- Duplicate values allowed; enum constants int type ke.\n### Related data-type facts\n- `sizeof(char)` = 1 hamesha; int typically 4.\n- Integer promotion: char/short arithmetic me int ban jaate.\n- `unsigned` aur signed mix → signed value unsigned me convert (−1 > 1u true!).","tips":"- WHITE = 2 ke baad BLACK = 3 — \"pichhle explicit value + 1\".","sol":"RED 0, GREEN 5, BLUE 6, WHITE 2, BLACK 3 → 6 + 3 = **9**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 9**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 2.3 (Constants, enumeration constants)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-48":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Generator = 1011 (degree 3) → 3 zeros jodo: 1011001000, phir modulo-2 (XOR) division.","concept":"### Cyclic Redundancy Check\n1. Generator degree r → message ke peeche r zeros.\n2. Modulo-2 (XOR) division by generator.\n3. Remainder (r bits) = CRC → message ke saath bhejo.\n4. Receiver: poora codeword ÷ generator; remainder 0 → no error.\n- Detects: sab single-bit errors (generator me ≥ 2 terms), sab odd errors (agar (x + 1) factor), burst ≤ r.\n- CRC-32 Ethernet me use.","tips":"- Leading 1 pe hi XOR karo; har step me ek bit aage badho.","sol":"Dividend = 1011001000 (data + 000).\n- Step 1: pehle 4 bits 1011 ⊕ 1011 = 0000 → ab bacha 0000001000.\n- Step 2: agla leading 1 position 7 pe: 1000 ⊕ 1011 = 0011 → bacha 0000000011.\n- Remainder (last 3 bits) = **011**.\nTransmitted codeword: 1011001 011 (= 1011001011, jo 1011 se poora divide hota hai).\n\n**Answer: (C) 011**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 10.4 (Cyclic codes, CRC encoder/decoder).\n- Tanenbaum — Computer Networks, Sec 3.2.2."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-49":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"ReLU = max(0, x) → range [0, ∞). (−1, 1) tanh ka hai.","concept":"### ANN essentials\n- Perceptron: linearly separable problems hi (AND, OR, NOT); XOR ke liye hidden layer.\n- **Perceptron convergence theorem**: separable data pe finite steps me converge.\n- Backprop: chain rule → derivative chahiye (step function nahi chalega).\n- Activations: sigmoid (0, 1), tanh (−1, 1), ReLU [0, ∞), softmax (probabilities).\n- Bias = threshold ko shift karta (w₀ with input 1).","tips":"- ANN passage/statements har haal ke paper me — ye 5 facts pakke rakho.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✓, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B), (D) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Simon Haykin — Neural Networks and Learning Machines, Ch 1 (Perceptron), Ch 4 (Multilayer perceptrons)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-50":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"n² ordered pairs; n diagonal pairs fixed (must include); baaki n² − n free.","concept":"### Counting relations on n-element set\n- Total: 2^(n²)\n- Reflexive: 2^(n² − n)\n- Irreflexive: 2^(n² − n)\n- Symmetric: 2^(n(n+1)/2)\n- Antisymmetric: 2^n · 3^(n(n−1)/2)\n- Reflexive + symmetric: 2^(n(n−1)/2)\n- Asymmetric: 3^(n(n−1)/2)\n- Equivalence relations = Bell number Bₙ (B₄ = 15).","tips":"- n = 4: n² − n = 12.","sol":"2^(16 − 4) = **2¹²** = 4096.\n\n**Answer: (C) 2¹²**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 9.1 (Relations, counting exercises)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-51":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Internal node: p pointers + (p − 1) keys ≤ block size → 8p + 10(p − 1) ≤ 1024.","concept":"### B / B⁺ tree order\n- **B⁺ internal node**: p·(block ptr) + (p − 1)·key ≤ B.\n- **B⁺ leaf node**: n·(key + record/data ptr) + 1 next-leaf ptr ≤ B.\n- **B-tree node**: p·(block ptr) + (p − 1)·(key + data ptr) ≤ B.\n- Min children (non-root) = ⌈p/2⌉.\n- B⁺: sab data leaves pe, leaves linked → range queries fast; internal nodes me sirf keys → zyada fan-out, kam height.","tips":"- Hamesha floor lo: 18p ≤ 1034 → p ≤ 57.44 → 57.","sol":"8p + 10p − 10 ≤ 1024 → 18p ≤ 1034 → p = **57**.\n(Check: 57 × 8 + 56 × 10 = 1016 ≤ 1024; 58 → 1034 > 1024.)\n\n**Answer: (B) 57**","refs":"- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 17.3–17.4 (B-trees and B⁺-trees, order calculation examples)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-52":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Instruction streams × data streams. Ek instruction, bahut data = SIMD (vector).","concept":"### Flynn's taxonomy\n- **SISD**: ek CPU, ek data — traditional uniprocessor.\n- **SIMD**: ek control unit, kai processing elements — vector/array processors, GPU (SIMT), SSE/AVX.\n- **MISD**: kai instructions ek data pe — practically rare (systolic arrays, fault-tolerant flight control).\n- **MIMD**: kai independent processors — multicore, clusters. Shared memory (UMA/NUMA) ya distributed memory.","tips":"- MISD ka example sabse kam common — elimination se last me fit karo.","sol":"SISD → III, SIMD → I, MISD → IV, MIMD → II.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 17.1 (Multiple processor organizations).\n- Kai Hwang — Advanced Computer Architecture, Sec 1.2."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-53":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"XML = data carry/describe, strict rules (quotes, closing tags, single root, case-sensitive). HTML = display.","concept":"### XML rules (well-formed)\n- Har start tag ka end tag (ya self-closing `<br/>`).\n- Proper nesting; exactly ek root element.\n- Case-sensitive (`<Book>` ≠ `<book>`).\n- Attribute values hamesha quotes me.\n- **Valid** XML = well-formed + DTD / XML Schema (XSD) ke according.\n- Related: XSLT (transform), XPath (navigate), DOM (tree API), SAX (event-based parser).","tips":"- (E) ulta likha hai — classic trap.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- W3C — Extensible Markup Language (XML) 1.0 Recommendation, Sec 2 (Documents, well-formedness).\n- Deitel & Deitel — Internet & World Wide Web: How to Program, XML chapter."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-54":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Generalised pigeonhole: N objects, k boxes → kisi box me ≥ ⌈N/k⌉. ⌈N/4⌉ ≥ 5 → N ≥ 17.","concept":"### Pigeonhole principle\n- N + 1 objects, N boxes → kisi box me ≥ 2.\n- **Generalised**: N objects, k boxes → ≥ ⌈N/k⌉.\n- \"Guarantee at least r in one box\" → minimum N = k(r − 1) + 1.\n- Examples: 367 logon me 2 ka birthday same (366 days); 13 logon me 2 ka birth month same.","tips":"- Worst case: har grade 4 students (16) → 17th student kisi grade ko 5 bana deta.","sol":"N = 4 × (5 − 1) + 1 = **17**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 17**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 6.2 (The pigeonhole principle)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-55":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Chhote se bade: module → modules ka combination → poora system → customer ka acceptance.","concept":"### Testing strategy (spiral, Pressman)\n- **Unit** (component-level, white-box heavy) → **Integration** (interfaces; top-down/bottom-up) → **Validation/System** (requirements, non-functional: stress, security, recovery, performance) → **Acceptance** (customer: alpha, beta).\n- Regression testing har change ke baad; smoke testing har build pe.","tips":"- Acceptance hamesha last.","sol":"Unit (B) → Integration (D) → System (A) → Acceptance (C).\n\n**Answer: (D) (B), (D), (A), (C)**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 22.1 (A strategic approach to software testing)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-56":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"NP-hard ⊄ NP zaroori nahi (halting problem NP-hard hai par NP me nahi, undecidable). 2-SAT polynomial (P me).","concept":"### Complexity classes\n- **P**: polynomial time me solve.\n- **NP**: polynomial time me verify (certificate).\n- **NP-hard**: har NP problem isme polynomial reduce ho; NP me hona zaroori nahi.\n- **NP-complete** = NP ∩ NP-hard. Pehla: SAT (Cook-Levin).\n- Known NPC: 3-SAT, Clique, Vertex cover, Hamiltonian cycle, TSP (decision), Subset sum, 0/1 knapsack (decision), Graph colouring (k ≥ 3).\n- **P me**: 2-SAT, 2-colouring, shortest path, MST, Euler circuit.\n- Reduction: A ≤p B aur A NP-hard → B NP-hard.","tips":"- \"Every NP-hard is in NP\" → galat (classic).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗, (C) ✓ (har NP problem halting problem me reduce ho sakta), (D) ✓, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Ch 34 (NP-completeness)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-57":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Dead code = jiska result kabhi use nahi hota / jo kabhi execute nahi hota.","concept":"### Machine-independent optimizations\n- **Constant folding / propagation**: `x = 3 * 4` → 12.\n- **Common subexpression elimination** (DAG se).\n- **Copy propagation**, **dead code elimination**.\n- **Loop optimizations**: code motion, strength reduction, induction variable elimination, loop unrolling, loop fusion.\n### Peephole (machine-dependent)\nRedundant load/store hatana, unreachable code, flow-of-control (jump to jump), algebraic simplification, machine idioms (INC).\n### Basic blocks\nLeaders: pehla statement, jump ka target, jump ke baad wala statement.","tips":"- Strength reduction = mehenga operation (×, ÷) → sasta (+, shift).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B), (C) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 8.7 (Peephole optimization), Sec 9.1 (Principal sources of optimization)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-58":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"802.3 Ethernet, 802.11 Wi-Fi, 802.15 personal area (Bluetooth .1, ZigBee .4), 802.16 WiMAX.","concept":"### IEEE 802 family\n- 802.2 LLC; 802.3 Ethernet (CSMA/CD); 802.4 token bus; 802.5 token ring.\n- 802.11 WLAN — MAC: **CSMA/CA** (RTS/CTS); a/b/g/n/ac/ax.\n- 802.15.1 Bluetooth (piconet: 1 master + 7 active slaves; scatternet); 802.15.4 ZigBee (IoT).\n- 802.16 WiMAX; 802.1Q VLAN tagging.\n### Wireless\n- Hidden terminal problem → RTS/CTS. Exposed terminal problem.\n- Cellular: GSM (TDMA + FDMA), CDMA (spread spectrum), LTE (OFDMA).","tips":"- Wi-Fi me CSMA/CA (collision avoidance), Ethernet me CSMA/CD (detection).","sol":"802.3 → III, 802.11 → I, 802.15.1 → IV, 802.16 → II.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch 13 (Wired LANs: Ethernet), Ch 15 (Wireless LANs, Bluetooth), Ch 16."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-59":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pehle → hatao (¬ andar le jaane ke liye zaroori), phir ¬ andar, phir variables alag naam, phir ∃ hatao, phir ∀ drop + distribute.","concept":"### Clause form conversion (Russell & Norvig)\n1. Eliminate ↔, →.\n2. Move ¬ inwards (De Morgan, ¬∀ ≡ ∃¬).\n3. Standardize variables (har quantifier ka alag variable).\n4. Skolemize: ∃x P(x) → P(A); ∀y∃x P(x, y) → P(F(y), y).\n5. Drop ∀.\n6. Distribute ∨ over ∧ → clauses.","tips":"- Standardize Skolemize se pehle — warna Skolem function galat variables pe depend kar sakta.","sol":"A → B → C → D → E.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B), (C), (D), (E)**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 9.5.1 (Conjunctive normal form for FOL)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-60":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Consensus theorem: AB + A'C + BC = AB + A'C (BC redundant).","concept":"### Boolean algebra laws\n- Identity, null, idempotent, complement laws.\n- Absorption: A + AB = A; A(A + B) = A.\n- **Redundancy**: A + A'B = A + B.\n- **Consensus**: AB + A'C + BC = AB + A'C; dual (A + B)(A' + C)(B + C) = (A + B)(A' + C).\n- De Morgan: (A + B)' = A'B'; (AB)' = A' + B'.\n- Duality: + ↔ ·, 0 ↔ 1.","tips":"- Consensus term = jo do terms ke \"complementary variable\" ko hata ke bane (A aur A' → B·C).","sol":"BC = BC(A + A') = ABC + A'BC; ABC ⊆ AB aur A'BC ⊆ A'C → BC redundant.\nF = **AB + A'C**.\n\n**Answer: (B) AB + A'C**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 2.4 (Basic theorems), consensus theorem."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-61":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Confidence(X → Y) = support(X ∪ Y) / support(X).","concept":"### Association rule mining\n- **Support(X)** = transactions containing X / total.\n- **Confidence(X → Y)** = σ(X ∪ Y) / σ(X).\n- **Lift** = confidence / support(Y); > 1 → positive correlation.\n- **Apriori property**: frequent itemset ke sab subsets frequent; infrequent ka koi superset frequent nahi → candidate pruning.\n- FP-Growth: candidate generation ke bina (FP-tree).\n### Data warehouse quick facts\n- OLAP operations: roll-up, drill-down, slice, dice, pivot.\n- Schemas: star (denormalised dimensions), snowflake (normalised), fact constellation.","tips":"- Pehle X ko gino (denominator), phir X ∪ Y.","sol":"{Pen, Notebook}: T1, T2, T5 → 3. {Pen, Notebook, Ink}: T1, T5 → 2.\nConfidence = 2/3 ≈ **0.67**. (Support of the rule = 2/5 = 0.40 — option A trap; support(Pen, Notebook) = 0.60 — option B trap.)\n\n**Answer: (C) 0.67**","refs":"- Tan, Steinbach, Kumar — Introduction to Data Mining, Sec 6.1 (Association analysis: support and confidence).\n- Han, Kamber — Data Mining: Concepts and Techniques, Ch 6."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-62":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Hardwired = fast par rigid; microprogrammed = flexible par slow (control memory access).","concept":"### Control unit design\n- **Hardwired**: gates/flip-flops/decoders se signals; fast; RISC me common; change karna mushkil.\n- **Microprogrammed**: control memory (ROM) me microprogram; CAR (control address register), CDR; flexible; CISC me common.\n- **Horizontal**: har control signal ka ek bit → wide word, high parallelism, no decoding.\n- **Vertical**: encoded fields → narrow word, decoder chahiye, kam parallelism.\n- Mano: 20-bit microinstruction = F1 F2 F3 (3 bits each) + CD (2) + BR (2) + AD (7).","tips":"- \"Hardwired slower\" → galat (classic trap).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B), (C) and (E) Only**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Ch 7 (Microprogrammed control).\n- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Ch 21 (Microprogrammed control)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-63":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Isometric = orthographic axonometric (projectors ⟂ plane), oblique nahi. Perspective me door ki cheez chhoti (foreshortening).","concept":"### Projections\n- **Parallel** (centre at infinity): proportions preserved, realistic nahi.\n  - **Orthographic** (projectors ⟂ plane): multiview (top, front, side) + **axonometric** (isometric, dimetric, trimetric).\n  - **Oblique** (projectors not ⟂): **cavalier** (depth full, 45°), **cabinet** (depth half, ~63.4°).\n- **Perspective** (finite centre): realistic; vanishing points (1-, 2-, 3-point); size ∝ 1/distance.\n- Isometric: teeno axes equally foreshortened (120° apart).","tips":"- Oblique ke sirf do naam yaad: cavalier, cabinet.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✓, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 12.3 (Projections: parallel, oblique, perspective)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-64":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pehle counting (UFP), phir adjustment (GSC → VAF), phir multiply.","concept":"### FP vs LOC\n- FP language-independent, requirements stage pe hi estimate.\n- LOC language-dependent, design/code ke baad accurate.\n- Productivity = FP / person-month; quality = defects / FP.\n- Backfiring: FP × (LOC per FP for language) → LOC.\n- Complexity weights (simple/avg/complex): EI 3/4/6, EO 4/5/7, EQ 3/4/6, ILF 7/10/15, EIF 5/7/10.","tips":"- VAF bina ΣFi ke nahi banta → C, D se pehle.","sol":"A (count) → B (UFP) → C (rate GSCs) → D (VAF) → E (FP).\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B), (C), (D), (E)**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 30.2.1 (Function-based metrics)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-65":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Preventive = future problems rokne ke liye restructuring/documentation. Reverse engineering: code → design (ulta direction).","concept":"### Maintenance types\n- Corrective (~20%): bugs.\n- Adaptive (~25%): environment changes.\n- **Perfective (~50%)**: naye features, performance — sabse bada hissa.\n- Preventive (~5%): maintainability badhana.\n### Re-engineering\nReverse engineering (code → design/specs), restructuring, forward engineering (design → new code).","tips":"- \"New features\" = perfective, preventive nahi.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Ch 36 (Maintenance and re-engineering).\n- Ian Sommerville — Software Engineering, Sec 9.3."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-66":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Slot 5 se 9 tak bhar jaayenge; probing wrap-around karke 0 aur phir 1 pe jaata hai.","concept":"### Collision resolution\n- **Linear probing**: h(k), h(k)+1, h(k)+2 … (mod m) → **primary clustering**.\n- **Quadratic probing**: h(k) + i² → secondary clustering.\n- **Double hashing**: h1(k) + i·h2(k) → best open addressing.\n- **Chaining**: har slot pe linked list; load factor α > 1 bhi chal sakta.\n- Expected probes (uniform hashing): unsuccessful ≤ 1/(1 − α).","tips":"- mod m wrap-around mat bhoolo: slot 9 ke baad slot 0.","sol":"25 → 5; 36 → 6; 47 → 7; 15 → 5 ✗, 6 ✗, 7 ✗ → 8; 26 → 6, 7, 8 ✗ → 9; 55 → 5…9 ✗ → 0; 65 → 5…9, 0 ✗ → **1**.\nFinal: [55, 65, –, –, –, 25, 36, 47, 15, 26].\n\n**Answer: (C) 1**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 11.4 (Open addressing)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-67":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ RE.","concept":"### Machines aur language classes\n- DFA = NFA → regular.\n- **DPDA** → DCFL (proper subset of CFL; e.g. wwᴿ DCFL nahi).\n- **NPDA** → CFL.\n- **LBA** → CSL.\n- **TM** (deterministic = non-deterministic) → RE; always-halting TM → recursive.\n- Determinism se power sirf PDA me ghatti hai.","tips":"- DPDA aur NPDA ka order hi asli test hai.","sol":"DFA (B) < DPDA (E) < NPDA (C) < LBA (D) < TM (A).\n\n**Answer: (A) (B), (E), (C), (D), (A)**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 6.4 (Deterministic PDAs), Ch 8.\n- Peter Linz — An Introduction to Formal Languages and Automata, Sec 11.4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-68":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"BGP = exterior gateway protocol (autonomous systems ke beech), path-vector.","concept":"### Routing protocols\n- **Distance vector** (Bellman-Ford): neighbours se table exchange; count-to-infinity → split horizon, poison reverse. Example: **RIP** (hop count, max 15, update 30 s, UDP 520).\n- **Link state** (Dijkstra): LSAs flood, poora topology map. Example: **OSPF** (areas, cost metric), IS-IS.\n- **Path vector**: **BGP** (inter-AS, EGP, TCP 179).\n- IGP: RIP, OSPF, EIGRP; EGP: BGP.","tips":"- \"Interior\" vs \"exterior\" — BGP hamesha exterior.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B), (C) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Ch 20 (Unicast routing: DV, LS, path vector; RIP, OSPF, BGP)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-69":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Word → sentence structure → literal meaning → context me intended meaning (+ discourse).","concept":"### NLP pipeline\n1. **Morphological / lexical**: tokens, stemming, lemmatization, POS tagging.\n2. **Syntactic**: grammar (CFG), parse tree; parsers (CYK, Earley, chart).\n3. **Semantic**: literal meaning; word sense disambiguation.\n4. **Discourse integration**: pichhle sentences se link (anaphora resolution: \"he\" kaun?).\n5. **Pragmatic**: real intention (\"Can you pass the salt?\" = request).\n### Ambiguity\nLexical (\"bank\"), syntactic (\"I saw the man with a telescope\"), semantic, referential.","tips":"- Pragmatic = **context / intention**.","sol":"A → II, B → IV, C → I, D → III.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Ch 23 (Natural language for communication).\n- Rich & Knight — Artificial Intelligence, Ch 15 (Natural language processing)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-70":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Tree me cycle nahi → odd cycle bhi nahi → bipartite. K₅ aur K₃,₃ non-planar.","concept":"### Graph facts\n- Tree: connected, acyclic, n − 1 edges; har do vertices ke beech unique path.\n- **Bipartite ⇔ koi odd cycle nahi ⇔ 2-colourable**.\n- **Euler circuit** ⇔ connected + sab degrees even; **Euler path** ⇔ 0 ya 2 odd vertices.\n- **Kuratowski**: planar ⇔ K₅ / K₃,₃ ka subdivision na ho.\n- Chromatic number: Kₙ → n; cycle C_n → 2 (even), 3 (odd); tree (n ≥ 2) → 2; planar ≤ 4.","tips":"- K₅: e = 10 > 3v − 6 = 9 → non-planar (formula se turant).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✓, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B), (D) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 10.2, 10.5, 10.7, 10.8, 11.1."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-71":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"3NF me BCNF tootne ke liye X → A chahiye jahan A prime ho par X superkey na ho. Agar har key single attribute hai to A khud key → X superkey → contradiction.","concept":"### Normal form facts (GATE 2022 se repeat-prone)\n- Two-attribute relation hamesha BCNF (koi bhi non-trivial FD ka LHS key hota hai).\n- 3NF + saari candidate keys simple (single attribute) ⇒ BCNF.\n- BCNF ⊂ 3NF ⊂ 2NF ⊂ 1NF.\n- 3NF decomposition: lossless + dependency preserving hamesha possible; BCNF: lossless hamesha, preservation nahi.\n- Sab attributes prime → at least 3NF (BCNF zaroori nahi).","tips":"- Ye \"statements\" GATE 2022 se UGC NET June 2025 me aa chuke hain — aise variants phir aa sakte.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 7.3 (BCNF, 3NF).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 14.5."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-72":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Structural = resource; data = operand dependency; control = branch; forwarding = data hazard ka ilaaj.","concept":"### Pipeline hazards\n- **Structural**: single memory port, ek ALU → duplicate resources / separate I & D caches.\n- **Data**: RAW (true dependency), WAR (anti), WAW (output) — in-order 5-stage me sirf RAW problem.\n- **Control**: branches → stall, branch prediction (static/dynamic, BTB), delayed branch.\n- **Forwarding**: EX/MEM → EX input; load-use hazard me phir bhi 1 stall.\n- CPI = 1 + stall cycles per instruction.","tips":"- RAW = \"read after write\" — sabse common data hazard.","sol":"A → IV, B → I, C → II, D → III.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)**","refs":"- Hennessy & Patterson — Computer Organization and Design, Sec 4.5 (Pipeline hazards), Sec 4.7–4.8.\n- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 14.4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-73":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Call by name (ALGOL 60) = macro jaisa substitution; har use pe dobara evaluate (Jensen's device).","concept":"### Parameter passing\n- **Call by value**: copy; callee ka change caller tak nahi (C default).\n- **Call by reference**: address; changes caller me dikhte (C++ &, Pascal var).\n- **Call by value-result (copy-in copy-out)**: copy in, return pe copy back (Ada in out); aliasing me reference se alag result.\n- **Call by name**: textual substitution, lazy re-evaluation (ALGOL 60).\n- **Call by need**: name + memoization (Haskell lazy).\n### Binding\nStatic (compile time) vs dynamic (run time) binding; static vs dynamic scoping.","tips":"- \"Re-evaluated every time\" = call by name; \"once, when needed\" = call by need.","sol":"Textual substitution + har baar evaluation → **call by name**.\n\n**Answer: (D) Call by name**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 1.6.6 (Parameter passing mechanisms).\n- Robert W. Sebesta — Concepts of Programming Languages, Sec 9.5 (Parameter-passing methods)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-74":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"\"Every / all\" = division. Division ko π, × aur − se bhi likh sakte hain: (sab possible pairs) − (actual pairs) = \"missing\" pairs; jinka koi pair missing hai unhe hata do.","concept":"### Division\n- R(A, B) ÷ S(B) = { a | har b ∈ S ke liye (a, b) ∈ R }.\n- R ÷ S = π_A(R) − π_A( (π_A(R) × S) − R ).\n- Divisor ke attributes dividend ka subset hone chahiye ((D) me ulta hai).\n- π_sid(Enrolled ⋈ Course) = \"kam se kam ek course\" wale students.\n- SQL: NOT EXISTS (course jisme student enrolled nahi).","tips":"- Ye GATE 2022 (Employee–Brand–Own) ka pattern hai jo UGC NET June 2025 me aaya — naye naamon ke saath phir aa sakta.","sol":"(A) ✓ division; (B) ✓ division ka expanded form; (C) ✗ \"at least one course\"; (D) ✗ operands ulte.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A) and (B) Only**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 6.1.3 (Division operation and its rewriting).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 8.3.4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-75":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Best → worst: Data < Stamp < Control < External < Common < Content.","concept":"### Coupling (low better)\n- **Data**: elementary parameters.\n- **Stamp**: poora data structure pass (sirf kuch fields use).\n- **Control**: flag pass karke doosre ka flow control.\n- **External**: external format/device/protocol share.\n- **Common**: global data share.\n- **Content**: doosre module ke andar ka code/data directly change (worst).\n### Cohesion (high better)\nFunctional > Sequential > Communicational > Procedural > Temporal > Logical > Coincidental.","tips":"- \"Global\" = common, \"flag\" = control, \"internal\" = content.","sol":"A → IV, B → III, C → II, D → I.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 12.3.6 (Functional independence).\n- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Sec 5.2 (Cohesion and coupling)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-76":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"n^(log₂2) = n; f(n) = n log n = n × log¹n → extended master theorem case 2: Θ(n log^(k+1) n).","concept":"### Master theorem — extended case 2\n- f(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a) logᵏ n), k ≥ 0 → T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a) log^(k+1) n).\n- Yahan basic master theorem ka case 3 apply nahi hota (n log n polynomially bada nahi hai n se).\n- Recursion tree: har level pe ≈ n log(n/2ⁱ) → log n levels → Σ ≈ n log² n / 2.\n- Other: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n/log n → Θ(n log log n).","tips":"- Recurrences 10/10 sessions me aaye hain — case 2 ka extended form pakka rakho.","sol":"a = 2, b = 2 → n^(log₂2) = n. f(n) = n log n = Θ(n · log¹ n) → k = 1 → T(n) = **Θ(n log² n)**.\n\n**Answer: (A) Θ(n log² n)**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 4.5 and Exercise 4.6-2 (extended master theorem)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-77":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"CFG: membership (CYK), emptiness, finiteness decidable. Ambiguity, universality (= Σ*), equivalence undecidable. DFA ke lagbhag sab decidable.","concept":"### Decision properties\n- **Regular**: membership, emptiness, finiteness, equivalence, universality — sab decidable.\n- **CFL**: membership ✓, emptiness ✓, finiteness ✓; ambiguity ✗, L = Σ* ✗, equivalence ✗, L₁ ∩ L₂ = ∅ ✗, regularity ✗.\n- **CSL**: membership ✓, emptiness ✗.\n- **RE (TM)**: Rice's theorem — non-trivial language properties undecidable.","tips":"- CFG ke liye \"3 ✓ (membership, emptiness, finiteness)\" ratta.","sol":"(A) ✓ (CYK), (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✗, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 7.4 (Decision properties of CFLs), Sec 9.5."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-78":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Nyquist: C = 2B log₂ L.","concept":"### Channel capacity\n- **Nyquist (noiseless)**: C = 2B log₂ L (L = signal levels).\n- **Shannon (noisy)**: C = B log₂(1 + SNR); SNR(dB) = 10 log₁₀ SNR.\n- Shannon upper limit, Nyquist batata kitne levels chahiye.\n- Baud rate (signals/sec) vs bit rate: bit rate = baud × log₂ L.\n- Example: 3000 Hz, SNR 3162 (35 dB) → C ≈ 34.86 kbps.","tips":"- log₂ 8 = 3 → 2 × 4000 × 3.","sol":"C = 2 × 4000 × log₂ 8 = 8000 × 3 = **24 kbps**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 24 kbps**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 3.5 (Data rate limits: Nyquist and Shannon)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-79":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"TCP ACK = agla byte jo B expect kar raha (cumulative). Gap ho to wahi number dobara (duplicate ACK).","concept":"### TCP sequence & acknowledgement\n- Sequence number = segment ke **pehle byte** ka number.\n- ACK number = next expected byte (cumulative).\n- Out-of-order segment aaye → duplicate ACK; 3 duplicate ACKs → fast retransmit.\n- Lost segment retransmit hoke aaye → ACK jump: yahan 2200 (1900 + 300).\n- SYN aur FIN ek-ek sequence number khaate.","tips":"- GATE me ye type baar-baar aaya hai; UGC NET bhi GATE ke networking sawaal uthata hai.","sol":"First segment: bytes 1000–1499 → ACK **1500**. Third (1900–2199) aaya par 1500–1899 missing → phir ACK **1500** (duplicate).\n\n**Answer: (B) 1500 and 1500**","refs":"- Kurose & Ross — Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach, Sec 3.5.2 (Sequence numbers and acknowledgement numbers), Sec 3.5.4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-80":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har tile ki current aur goal position compare karo. Misplaced: 2, 8, 1, 6.","concept":"### Heuristics for the 8-puzzle\n- h₁ = misplaced tiles; h₂ = Manhattan distance — dono admissible; h₂ ≥ h₁ → h₂ dominates (A* kam nodes expand karta).\n- Relaxed problems se heuristics: tile kahin bhi ja sake → h₁; adjacent square me ja sake → h₂.\n- Effective branching factor b* se heuristic quality maapte.\n- Blank ko kabhi count nahi karte (warna admissibility toot sakti).","tips":"- 8 ka distance 2 (upar se neeche-left: 1 row + 1 column).","sol":"Misplaced: 2 (goal (1,2)), 8 (goal (2,1)), 1 (goal (1,1)), 6 (goal (3,2)); 3, 4, 7, 5 sahi → h₁ = **4**.\nManhattan: 2 → 1, 8 → 2, 1 → 1, 6 → 1 → h₂ = **5**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 4 and 5**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 3.6 (Heuristic functions for the 8-puzzle).\n- Rich & Knight — Artificial Intelligence, Ch 3."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-81":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"∀ distributes over ∧, ∃ distributes over ∨. Ulta (∀ over ∨, ∃ over ∧) sirf one-way implication.","concept":"### Quantifier laws\n- De Morgan: ¬∀x P ≡ ∃x ¬P; ¬∃x P ≡ ∀x ¬P.\n- ∀x(P ∧ Q) ≡ ∀xP ∧ ∀xQ; ∃x(P ∨ Q) ≡ ∃xP ∨ ∃xQ.\n- ∃x(P ∧ Q) ⇒ ∃xP ∧ ∃xQ (ulta false: koi even hai, koi odd hai, par koi dono nahi).\n- ∀xP ∨ ∀xQ ⇒ ∀x(P ∨ Q) (ulta false: har number even ya odd, par na sab even, na sab odd).\n- Counter-example soch ke check: integers pe P = even, Q = odd.","tips":"- Even/odd counter-example dono galat options ko ek saath tod deta.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✗, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 1.4 (Logical equivalences involving quantifiers)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-82":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Levels: internal (physical, lowest) → conceptual (logical) → external (views, highest).","concept":"### ANSI/SPARC three-schema architecture\n- **Internal**: storage structures, indexes, access paths.\n- **Conceptual**: entities, relationships, constraints (poore DB ka logical structure).\n- **External**: user-specific views.\n- **Physical data independence**: internal change → conceptual unaffected (easier).\n- **Logical data independence**: conceptual change → external views/programs unaffected (harder).\n- Mappings: external/conceptual, conceptual/internal.\n### DBMS languages\nDDL (schema), DML (procedural vs declarative), DCL; data dictionary = metadata.","tips":"- \"Lowest level\" = physical/internal, view hamesha highest.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✗ (ye internal schema), (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 2.2 (Three-schema architecture and data independence).\n- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 1.3."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-83":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"T0: AR ← PC; T1: IR ← M[AR], PC ← PC + 1; T2: decode; T3 onwards: (indirect) + execute.","concept":"### Mano basic computer — instruction cycle\n- T0: AR ← PC\n- T1: IR ← M[AR], PC ← PC + 1\n- T2: D0…D7 ← decode IR(12–14), AR ← IR(0–11), I ← IR(15)\n- T3: indirect ho to AR ← M[AR]; D7 = 1 → register-reference (I = 0) ya I/O (I = 1)\n- Interrupt cycle: R = 1 → RT0: AR ← 0, TR ← PC; RT1: M[AR] ← TR, PC ← 0; RT2: PC ← PC + 1, IEN ← 0, R ← 0.","tips":"- PC increment fetch ke saath hi (T1) hota hai.","sol":"A (T0) → B (T1) → C (T2) → D (T3 aage).\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B), (C), (D)**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 5.5 (Instruction cycle), Fig 5-9."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-84":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Macro expand karo: ((i++) > (j) ? (i++) : (j)). Condition true hai, to i++ **do baar** chalta hai.","concept":"### Macros vs functions\n- `#define` preprocessor ka text substitution — koi type checking nahi, arguments evaluate nahi hote, sirf copy hote.\n- Argument agar macro body me do baar aaye to uska side effect bhi do baar.\n- `?:` me condition ke baad sequence point hai → yahan behaviour well-defined.\n- Function `max(i++, j)` hota to i sirf ek baar badhta → k = 5, i = 6 (option A trap).\n- Inline function: type-safe alternative. `#` stringizing, `##` token pasting.","tips":"- Macro ke andar ++/-- wale arguments → hamesha expand karke gino.","sol":"(i++) > (j): 5 > 3 true, i = 6.\nPhir (i++) value 6 deta, i = 7 → k = 6.\nOutput: **6 7**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 6 7**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 4.11.2 (Macro substitution — pitfalls with side effects), Sec 2.11 (Conditional expressions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-85":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"RAID 0 = sirf striping (koi redundancy nahi); mirroring RAID 1 hai.","concept":"### Disk scheduling (8/10 sessions me aaya)\n- FCFS; SSTF (starvation); SCAN/elevator; C-SCAN (circular, uniform wait); LOOK, C-LOOK.\n- Seek time dominant; rotational latency avg = ½ rotation.\n### RAID\n- 0 striping; 1 mirroring; 2 Hamming; 3 byte + parity disk; 4 block + parity disk; 5 distributed parity; 6 dual parity; 10 = 1 + 0.","tips":"- \"RAID 0 redundancy\" → galat (0 = zero redundancy yaad rakho).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B), (C) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 11.2 (HDD scheduling), Sec 11.8 (RAID)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-86":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Basic COCOMO ka input KLOC hai. Log badhane se time linearly kam nahi hota (Brooks' law).","concept":"### COCOMO (Boehm)\n- Basic: E = a(KLOC)^b, D = c(E)^d. Organic (2.4, 1.05), semi-detached (3.0, 1.12), embedded (3.6, 1.20).\n- Intermediate: E × EAF, 15 cost drivers (product, hardware, personnel, project attributes).\n- Detailed: phase-wise cost drivers.\n- COCOMO II: application composition (object points), early design, post-architecture.\n- Brooks' law: late project me log jodne se aur late.","tips":"- Exponent sabse bada → embedded (1.20).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 33.6 (COCOMO II).\n- Barry Boehm — Software Engineering Economics, Ch 5–8."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-87":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Sort → pick → cycle check → add/union → repeat till V − 1 edges.","concept":"### MST algorithms\n- **Kruskal**: edge-based greedy; union-find; O(E log E) = O(E log V); sparse graphs ke liye achha; forest grow hota.\n- **Prim**: vertex-based; ek tree grow; binary heap O(E log V), array O(V²) (dense).\n- **Cut property**: kisi cut ka minimum crossing edge MST me.\n- Distinct weights → MST unique.\n- MST me edges = V − 1.","tips":"- Cycle check **add** karne se pehle — (D) se pehle (C).","sol":"A → B → C → D → E.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B), (C), (D), (E)**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 23.2 (Kruskal and Prim)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-88":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"g ∘ f injective ⇒ f injective (g nahi). g ∘ f onto ⇒ g onto (f nahi).","concept":"### Functions\n- Composition of injections/surjections/bijections → same type.\n- g ∘ f injective ⇒ f injective; g ∘ f surjective ⇒ g surjective.\n- |B|^|A| total functions; injections P(n, m); surjections inclusion–exclusion.\n- Finite set to itself: injective ⇔ surjective ⇔ bijective (pigeonhole).\n- Inverse exists ⇔ bijection.","tips":"- Yaad: \"inner (f) ka one-one, outer (g) ka onto\".","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✓ (2³), (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B), (D) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 2.3 (Functions), exercises on composition."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-89":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"IaaS = virtual machines (EC2); PaaS = app deploy platform (App Engine, Heroku); SaaS = ready app (Gmail); FaaS = functions on events (Lambda).","concept":"### Cloud computing (NIST)\n- **Service models**: IaaS (user manages OS upwards), PaaS (user manages app + data), SaaS (provider manages all).\n- **Deployment models**: public, private, community, hybrid.\n- **Essential characteristics**: on-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling, rapid elasticity, measured service.\n- Examples: IaaS — EC2, Azure VMs, GCE; PaaS — App Engine, Heroku, Azure App Service; SaaS — Salesforce, Office 365.\n### IoT\nSensors → gateway → cloud; protocols MQTT, CoAP; ZigBee, BLE.","tips":"- Control decreasing: IaaS > PaaS > SaaS (user ke haath me).","sol":"IaaS → EC2 (III), PaaS → App Engine (IV), SaaS → Google Workspace (I), FaaS → Lambda (II).\n\n**Answer: (B) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- NIST SP 800-145 — The NIST Definition of Cloud Computing.\n- Rajkumar Buyya — Mastering Cloud Computing, Ch 4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-90":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"110|01 aur 001|10 → tails swap: 110 + 10, 001 + 01.","concept":"### Genetic operators\n- **Single-point crossover**: ek point ke baad ke genes swap.\n- **Two-point**: do points ke beech ka segment swap.\n- **Uniform**: har gene randomly kisi parent se (mask).\n- **Mutation**: bit flip (probability chhoti).\n- **Selection**: roulette wheel (fitness proportionate), tournament, rank; elitism.\n- Schema theorem (Holland): short, low-order, above-average schemata exponentially badhte.","tips":"- Head same rehta, tail exchange hota.","sol":"Parent1 = 110|01, Parent2 = 001|10 → Child1 = 110 10 = **11010**, Child2 = 001 01 = **00101**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 11010 and 00101**","refs":"- David E. Goldberg — Genetic Algorithms in Search, Optimization and Machine Learning, Ch 1.\n- S. N. Sivanandam — Principles of Soft Computing, Ch 15 (Genetic algorithms)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-91":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Gantt: P1 (0–1), P2 (1–3), P3 (3–5), P2 (5–8), P4 (8–14), P1 (14–22).","concept":"### SRTF (preemptive SJF)\n- Har arrival pe check: naye process ka burst < current ka remaining → preempt.\n- Average waiting time **minimum** (sab algorithms me), par burst ka pata hona chahiye; long jobs starve.\n- **Waiting time = Turnaround − Burst**, **Turnaround = Completion − Arrival**.","tips":"- t = 3 pe P3 (2) < P2 remaining (3) → P3 preempt karta.","sol":"Completion: P3 = 5, P2 = 8, P4 = 14, P1 = 22.\nWT: P1 = 22 − 0 − 9 = 13; P2 = 8 − 1 − 5 = 2; P3 = 5 − 3 − 2 = 0; P4 = 14 − 4 − 6 = 4.\nAverage = (13 + 2 + 0 + 4)/4 = 19/4 = **4.75 ms**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 4.75 ms**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 5.3.2 (Shortest-job-first / SRTF scheduling)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-92":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Order P1, P2, P3, P4: start times 0, 9, 14, 16.","concept":"### FCFS\n- Non-preemptive, arrival order.\n- **Convoy effect**: lamba process aage ho to chhote sab wait karte → average WT bada.\n- Waiting time = start time − arrival time (non-preemptive me).","tips":"- Gantt likho: P1 0–9, P2 9–14, P3 14–16, P4 16–22.","sol":"WT: P1 = 0; P2 = 9 − 1 = 8; P3 = 14 − 3 = 11; P4 = 16 − 4 = 12.\nAverage = 31/4 = **7.75 ms**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 7.75 ms**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 5.3.1 (FCFS scheduling)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-93":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"t = 0 pe sirf P1 → P1 poora (0–9). Phir ready: P3 (2), P2 (5), P4 (6).","concept":"### Non-preemptive SJF\n- CPU free hone par ready queue ka sabse chhota burst.\n- Running process ko beech me nahi rokte (SRTF se farq).\n- Tie → FCFS.","tips":"- SRTF ka average TAT (10.25) aur FCFS ka (13.25) options me trap hain.","sol":"Gantt: P1 0–9, P3 9–11, P2 11–16, P4 16–22.\nTAT: P1 = 9; P3 = 11 − 3 = 8; P2 = 16 − 1 = 15; P4 = 22 − 4 = 18.\nAverage = 50/4 = **12.5 ms**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 12.5 ms**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 5.3.2 (Shortest-job-first scheduling)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-94":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Gantt: P1 0–3, P2 3–6, P3 6–8, P1 8–11, P4 11–14, P2 14–16, P1 16–19, P4 19–22.","concept":"### Round Robin\n- Ready queue FIFO; har process ko max q time; khatam na ho to queue ke end me.\n- q bahut bada → FCFS; bahut chhota → context switch overhead.\n- Response time achha; average WT aksar SJF se zyada.\n- Tie rule (passage me diya): quantum khatam hone ke instant aaya naya process pehle queue me.","tips":"- t = 3 pe queue: P2, P3 (abhi aaya), P1 (preempted). P4 t = 4 pe P1 ke peeche lagta.","sol":"Completion: P3 = 8, P2 = 16, P1 = 19, P4 = 22.\nWT = completion − arrival − burst: P1 = 19 − 0 − 9 = 10; P2 = 16 − 1 − 5 = 10; P3 = 8 − 3 − 2 = 3; P4 = 22 − 4 − 6 = 12.\nAverage = 35/4 = **8.75 ms**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 8.75 ms**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 5.3.3 (Round-robin scheduling)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-95":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"P1 sirf 1 ms chala, phir P2, P3, P4 sab chhote the; P1 ka bacha 8 ms sabse last me.","concept":"### Gantt chart se answer padhna\n- Completion time = process ka **last** segment kab khatam hua.\n- Preemptive scheduling me ek process ke kai segments ho sakte.\n- Response time = pehli baar CPU mila − arrival (P1 ka 0).\n- Starvation ka example: lamba job baar-baar chhote jobs ke piche.","tips":"- t = 14 pe sirf P1 bacha → 14 + 8 = 22.","sol":"SRTF Gantt: P1 0–1, P2 1–3, P3 3–5, P2 5–8, P4 8–14, P1 14–22.\nP1 completion = **22 ms**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 22 ms**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 5.3.2."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-96":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"DFA ko yaad rakhna hai: \"abhi tak ka suffix kuch nahi / 0 / 01\". Teen alag situations.","concept":"### \"Ends with w\" DFA\n- |w| = k → minimum DFA states = k + 1 (pattern ke prefixes jitne).\n- Myhill–Nerode: equivalence classes ε, \"0\", \"01\" → 3.\n- Transitions (q0 = no progress, q1 = last 0, q2 = last 01 final):\n  q0 –0→ q1, q0 –1→ q0; q1 –0→ q1, q1 –1→ q2; q2 –0→ q1, q2 –1→ q0.","tips":"- \"Ends with\" → k + 1; \"contains\" substring → k + 1 bhi (dead/trap final state ke saath).","sol":"Classes: (i) koi progress nahi, (ii) last symbol 0, (iii) last two \"01\" (accept) → **3 states**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 3**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 2.2, Sec 4.4 (Minimization, Myhill–Nerode)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-97":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Kuch bhi + aakhir me 01.","concept":"### Regular expression patterns\n- Starts with w: w(0 + 1)*\n- Ends with w: (0 + 1)*w\n- Contains w: (0 + 1)*w(0 + 1)*\n- Even length: ((0 + 1)(0 + 1))*\n- No two consecutive 1s: (0 + 10)*(ε + 1)\n- Identities: (r*)* = r*, (r + s)* = (r*s*)*, r*r = rr* = r⁺.","tips":"- (01)* \"ends with 01\" ke alawa ε bhi deta aur 1101 nahi deta → galat.","sol":"L = sab strings jinka suffix 01 → **(0 + 1)*01**.\n\n**Answer: (C) (0 + 1)*01**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 3.1 (Regular expressions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-98":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Last 2 bits fixed (01), pehle 3 bits free → 2³.","concept":"### Counting strings in a language\n- Fixed suffix/prefix of length k, total n → 2^(n − k).\n- Contains substring → complement count karo (aksar recurrence se).\n- No two consecutive 1s, length n → Fibonacci F(n + 2) (n = 5 → 13).","tips":"- Length 2 → sirf \"01\" (1 string) — formula check: 2⁰ = 1.","sol":"xxx01 → 2³ = **8** strings.\n\n**Answer: (D) 8**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 6.1 (Product rule)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-99":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"q0 (0, 1 pe loop) –0→ q1 –1→ q2 (final). 2 states me \"01\" ki do-step memory nahi ban sakti.","concept":"### NFA vs DFA size\n- NFA aksar DFA se chhota; kabhi exponential chhota (\"k-th symbol from end is 1\": NFA k + 1, DFA 2^k).\n- Lower bound trick: shortest accepted string ki length ℓ ho to NFA me kam se kam ℓ + 1 states — us path pe koi state repeat hui to loop kaat ke aur chhoti accepted string mil jaati (contradiction).\n- Subset construction: NFA → DFA, worst case 2^n states.","tips":"- Shortest string \"01\" length 2 → kam se kam 3 states.","sol":"NFA: q0 –0,1→ q0, q0 –0→ q1, q1 –1→ q2 (final) → 3 states; shortest string length 2 hai isliye 2 states se kam nahi ho sakta → **3**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 3**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 2.3 (NFA, Example 2.6 — strings ending in 01)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s1-100":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pehle check \"ends with 01\", phir 1s gino.","concept":"### Product construction\n- L₁ ∩ L₂ (dono regular) → product DFA: states = pairs; final = dono final.\n- Yahan L (3 states) × even-1s DFA (2 states) → max 6 states.\n- Regular languages ∩, ∪, complement, difference sab ke under closed.","tips":"- 0110 aur 111 \"01\" pe khatam hi nahi hote → turant out.","sol":"- 1001: ends 01 ✓, 1s = 2 (even) ✓ → **in L ∩ even-1s**.\n- 1101: ends 01 ✓, 1s = 3 ✗.\n- 0110, 111: L me nahi.\n\n**Answer: (A) 1001**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 4.2.1 (Closure under intersection — product construction)."}});
